{%- comment -%}
  THP — the canonical Shop by Colour palette.

  The homepage and the /pages/shop-by-colour page are two instances of the same
  section, and Shopify keeps block settings per TEMPLATE. So while the palette
  lived in section blocks it existed twice, and the two copies drifted: the
  first swatch was renamed to "Yellow" on the homepage and stayed "Whimsy
  Yellow" on the page. Liquid cannot read another template's blocks, so no
  amount of section code could reconcile them.

  Worse, templates/*.json is effectively owned by the store — it arrives here
  through the automatic "Update from Shopify" commits — so editing the page
  template in this repo would simply be overwritten on the next sync.

  Both instances render sections/hp-shop-by-color.liquid, which IS owned by this
  repo, so the palette lives here instead and there is only ever one of it.
  Presentation stays per instance: eyebrow, heading, intro text, columns,
  product counts and the default swatch are all still section settings, and the
  two pages legitimately differ on several of them.

  TO ADD, RENAME OR REORDER A COLOUR, edit the rows below and push the theme.
  Order here is the order the swatches appear in.

    Title | Swatch hex | Product tag | Aliases | View all URL

  Only Title and Swatch hex are required.

  - Product tag  defaults to the handleized title when blank, and is what the
                 section matches against product tags and the Color category
                 metafield.
  - Aliases      comma-separated other names this colour goes by, matched
                 against titles, variant options and metafield entries. This is
                 the no-rename escape hatch: "Limone Yellow" on the palette is
                 "Limone Gardenia" on the shelf.
  - View all URL overrides where the swatch's "View all" link points. Leave
                 blank to use the section's own "View all goes to" setting.

  Rows are separated by ";" and fields by "|", because Liquid has no way to
  express a list of records. Rendered as data and captured by the section — a
  snippet is used rather than {% include %} so nothing leaks into the section's
  scope beyond the string itself.
{%- endcomment -%}
  Yellow                  | #e3c10c | yellow;
